  <h1>India’s Agriculture Reforms — From MSP to Market Linkages</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Agriculture sustains nearly 45% of India’s workforce but contributes only ~18% to GDP. In 2024, Indian Express discussed the continuing debate on Minimum Support Price (MSP), market reforms, and diversification, noting that while the government introduced several measures, structural challenges persist. This issue is central for UPSC as it combines economy, governance, and social justice.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status of MSP<br/>•	MSP Coverage: Declared for 23 crops but procurement effectively limited to wheat, rice, and cotton.<br/>•	Farmers’ Demand: Legal guarantee of MSP for all crops.<br/>•	Government View: Legal MSP seen as fiscally unsustainable and distortionary.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Market Reforms and Initiatives<br/>1.	e-NAM (Electronic National Agriculture Market): Online trading platform linking 1,000+ mandis.<br/>2.	Contract Farming: Encouraged through Model Act (though repealed 2020 farm laws stalled reforms).<br/>3.	Farmer Producer Organizations (FPOs): Target of 10,000 FPOs to improve bargaining power.<br/>4.	Infrastructure Push: Agri-infra fund, cold storage, and logistics under PM Gati Shakti.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Diversification and Sustainability<br/>•	Millets Push: Declared International Year of Millets (2023); promoted as climate-resilient crops.<br/>•	Crop Diversification: Encouraged away from paddy and sugarcane in water-stressed areas.<br/>•	Agri-Tech: Use of drones, AI, and precision farming promoted.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Challenges<br/>•	Price Volatility: Farmers vulnerable to global and domestic shocks.<br/>•	Regional Disparities: MSP benefits concentrated in Punjab–Haryana belt.<br/>•	Low Storage Capacity: ~30% food loss due to poor post-harvest management.<br/>•	Climate Change: Erratic rainfall, heatwaves reducing yields.<br/>•	Small Holdings: 85% farmers are small/marginal, limiting economies of scale.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Food Security: MSP procurement critical for PDS.<br/>•	Rural Livelihoods: Agriculture employs nearly half of workforce.<br/>•	Political Economy: Farmers’ protests shape electoral politics.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-III (Economy & Agriculture): MSP, reforms, FPOs, food security.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Centre–State disputes over reforms.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Agriculture in India is not just an economic activity but a way of life.”<br/>o	“Doubling farmers’ income: vision vs reality.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: State–society relations; political mobilization of farmers.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	MSP Reform: Targeted MSP + direct income support (like PM-KISAN) instead of blanket MSP.<br/>2.	Strengthen FPOs: Scale up collective bargaining, direct market access.<br/>3.	Agri-Infrastructure: Expand cold chains, warehouses, rural logistics.<br/>4.	Climate-Resilient Farming: Invest in drought-resistant seeds, water-saving techniques.<br/>5.	Integrated Reforms: Combine procurement, insurance (PMFBY), and agri-tech adoption.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>Agriculture reforms remain one of India’s toughest policy challenges — balancing farmer welfare, fiscal sustainability, and market efficiency. The way forward lies in diversifying crops, empowering smallholders, and building resilient value chains. For UPSC aspirants, agriculture illustrates how economics, politics, and social justice intersect in policy-making.</p>
